"# Chapter 2 — There is no import statement

Here is the most important sentence in this book: **Motoi has no `import` statement.** If you came from a language with a module system — Python, Racket, R6RS Scheme, JavaScript — this will feel like a missing tooth. It is not missing. It was never there, and it is not coming. This chapter is about why, and about what you do instead (which is: nothing — you just call the verb).

## 2.1 A dialect boundary, named out loud

In some other Schemes you write a line at the top of a file to pull a library in. In R6RS you might write `(import (rnrs))`. In R7RS, `(import (scheme base))`. Racket has `require`. Those forms locate a library, evaluate it, and bind its names into your program.

Motoi does none of that. There is no `(import ...)` special form. If you type one, Motoi tells you the truth:

Typing `(import manners)` at a Motoi prompt yields `error: unbound symbol: import`. There is no such verb, because there is no such idea in this dialect. The word `import` is a boundary marker: cross it and you have left Motoi and entered some other Scheme.

That error is not a bug. It is the language telling you that you reached for a tool from a different dialect. This whole chapter lives on that boundary on purpose.

## 2.2 Batteries included — the real story

Motoi is *batteries-included*. Its libraries — lists (SRFI-1), strings (SRFI-13), hash tables (SRFI-125), randomness (SRFI-27), and Motoi's own runtime verbs — are loaded for you when the interpreter starts. You do not ask for them. They are simply there.

So the honest answer to *how do I get a library's verbs?* is: **you already have them. Call the verb.**

```scheme
(string-upcase \"hello\")
;; ⇒ \"HELLO\"
```

No import line preceded that. `string-upcase` comes from the SRFI-13 string library, and it was available the moment Motoi booted. The same is true of list verbs:

```scheme
(delete-duplicates (list 1 1 2 3 3 3))
;; ⇒ (1 2 3)
```

And of the randomness library:

```scheme
(list (integer? (random-integer 6))
      (number? (random)))
;; ⇒ (#t #t)
```

Three different libraries, three verbs, zero import lines. That is the whole of Motoi's \"module system\" from a caller's point of view: the verbs are loaded; you call them.

## 2.3 Why no import? Because the base is not small in that way

Chapter 1's story was that a small base plus optional modules keeps programs lean. Motoi makes a different bargain. Its standard library is fixed and known — R7RS-small plus a named set of SRFI parts — and the runtime brings those parts in for you. There is no menu to order from at the top of every file, because the meal is already on the table.

This trades one kind of leanness (only-pay-for-what-you-import) for another kind of simplicity (nothing to import, nothing to get wrong, nothing to pin). For a language meant to be learned on day one and used by kids and beginners, \"the verbs are just there\" is the friendlier deal.

## 2.4 What replaces `:only`, `:rename`, and `:as`

Other dialects' `import` forms carry knobs: `:only` to take a subset, `:rename` to dodge a collision, `:as` to hide names behind a prefix. Motoi has no import, so it has none of these knobs. What do you do instead?

- **Instead of `:only`** — you do nothing. Unused verbs cost you nothing; they simply sit in the runtime. You call the two you want and ignore the rest.
- **Instead of `:rename`** — you use `define`. If a name is awkward, bind a new one to it, right in your program:

```scheme
(define shout string-upcase)
(shout \"quiet please\")
;; ⇒ \"QUIET PLEASE\"
```

- **Instead of `:as` prefixing** — Motoi's own library verbs already carry a prefix in their name (`cortex/recall`, `hash-table-ref/default`). The namespacing is baked into the verb name, not applied at an import site.

So every job the four import shapes did in another dialect is either unnecessary here (`:only`) or done with a plain `define` (`:rename`), or already handled by naming convention (`:as`).

## 2.5 Reading a Motoi program's top

In an import-having language, the top of a file is a manifest: read the imports and you know the dependencies. A Motoi file has no such header, because every Motoi program depends on the same thing — the standard library, which is always present.

```scheme
;; No import block. Just code.
(define (greet name)
  (string-append \"Hello, \" name \".\"))

(greet \"Alfred\")
;; ⇒ \"Hello, Alfred.\"
```

`string-append` was available with no ceremony. That is the shape of every Motoi program: the code starts at the top, because there is nothing to declare first.

## 2.6 The one honest caveat

\"Batteries included\" means the *standard library* is always loaded. It does not mean every verb in the universe is loaded. Motoi's world is the R7RS-small core plus its adopted SRFI parts plus the Motoi runtime verbs. If you reach for a name outside that world, you get the same honest error you got for `import`. Typing `(module-verbs 'anything)` yields `error: unbound symbol: module-verbs` — because `module-verbs` is another dialect's introspection verb, not Motoi's. When Motoi does not have something, it says so plainly rather than pretending. That honesty *is* the feature.

## 2.7 What to try after this chapter

Open a Motoi file and try to call three library verbs — one string verb, one list verb, one random verb — with no setup line at all. Watch them all work. Then type `(import whatever)` and read the error. Sit with both facts at once: the verbs are there without importing, and `import` itself is not a Motoi word.

That is the muscle to build: stop scanning for an import header, and stop reaching for `import` to \"turn a library on.\" In Motoi the library is already on.

Turn the page. Chapter 3 covers the other half of this boundary — export — which Motoi also does not have, and for the same reason.")
